ex may blow but perhaps limited to O(26^2)? Need analyze.\n\nLet\'s understand effect of one char. Greedy partitions are segments where each segment is maximal prefix with <=k distinct, so next character would introduce (k+1)-th distinct. Changing one char can have local effect. It can alter the content and boundary of one segment, and subsequent boundaries shift/changes? It can cascade: changing char can remove a distinct that delayed boundary, making next segment include later chars, potentially shift all boundaries, causing many changes? Example k=1 and duplicate runs. Change one char can split a run, increasing partitions by at most? For k=1, changing one char can increase partitions by at most 1? Example "xxyz" -> "xayz" increases from 3? Let\'s compute original "xxyz", k=1: greedy "xx" (x), "y", "z": 3. Change x at index1 to a: "xayz": each char distinct adjacent? "x","a","y","z" =4, +1. Could changing one char increase many? String all same length n, k=1 -> one partition. Change one interior to b: "aaa b aaa". Greedy: "aaa", "b", "aaa" =3, +2. More? one b creates two extra boundaries. Max +2 likely. If change a char to create alternating? only one char, not many.\n\nMaybe answer differs from original by at most 2? If so compute original and test changes locally. Is that true? Changing one character can alter partition boundaries cascading but number of partitions maybe changes by small constant? Let\'s test. For k=2, string with repeated pattern maybe change can cause many segment length shifts and partition count change more? Suppose each segment starts due to exceeding k. Changing one char could remove a rare character that caused cut, merging segments, maybe decreases, not increase. To increase partitions, change a char may introduce new distinct earlier causing cut earlier. That can shift all subsequent boundaries, but total number partitions might vary. Could one insertion cause many extra partitions? Consider all \'a\'s, k=2. Original one partition. Change a middle to b -> "aaa b aaa": with k=2, distinct a,b <=2, still one partition. Change to b and c? only one char. k=1 yielded +2 only because segments of same char cannot include other chars. For k=3, all a, change to b => one partition. Need string already has k distinct; changing a char to (k+1) may cause split, but after split, maybe subsequent pattern? Could cascade? Let\'s attempt k=2. String = aaa bbb aaa ccc? Original? We need changing one char creates earlier cut, then subsequent segments maybe more numerous due to shifted boundaries, but each segment endpoints determined by first occurrence of third distinct. A single char could be the third distinct for many windows after shift? There is algorithmic need.\n\nCould brute explore small n to see difference max. But in reasoning no execution. Let\'s manually.\n\nk=2, original strings with groups? Greedy segments length until 3 distinct. A cut resets. Changing one char can modify at most a window until? After cut, reset at point; effect may disappear after passing original cut? Since change is one char, after that char leaves current segment, subsequent text same but start position may differ due to previous cut. Boundary shifts can persist.\n\nExample k=1: changing one char creates immediate cut before it and after, but then subsequent starts one char after? If original all a, after b at pos m, new boundaries: [0..m-1] a, [m] b, [m+1..n-1] a => +2. Effect on partition count bounded by +2. For k=2, changing one char might create a boundary if it becomes third distinct in current partition; then reset after it? Actually cut before changed char, new segment starts with changed char. Following segments start same index? Original may not have cut there. The reset can change boundaries downstream, but maybe number of extra cuts caused by one change <=2? Let\'s test.\n\nTake k=2, string mostly two letters alternating, original one partition. Change one char to third letter. String has third at pos m. Greedy: before m, a,b. At m c => exceed, cut before m. New segment starts c. Then following a,b can join until c? Since c at start, plus a,b <=2? Wait c,a,b are 3 if next both a and b appear within segment. The original changed char is still in current new segment as c. It will cause another cut when both a and b appear after m, because c+a+b=3. So changed char\'s c remains in current segment until a cut; after cut, c forgotten. This can cause an extra early cut at m, and then maybe original partitions shifted. Could c being at segment start alter first segment after m, but once cut, normal. Number extra maybe 2.\n\nLet\'s construct string after m has a,b,a,b... Starting original no c. At m c. Greedy:\nSegment before m: a,b... until m, cut count 1.\nNew segment starts c at m. It includes c and following chars until it encounters both a and b? Since c plus a =>2, then b =>3 cut before b. So very short: [c,a]? Actually if sequence after m is a,b, segment c,a (distinct c,a), next b introduces b third, cut before b. Segment [b,a...] without c normal. So the c introduced an extra cut near m, then reset. Extra partitions maybe +2: cut before m, and cut before b. Original had maybe no cut around. But after c forgotten, the suffix starts at b, whereas original segment at m would have started earlier. This can shift and change count by more? It effectively discards a prefix [m..b-1] as one segment, then suffix aligned one position b. Original at same point may be inside a long segment. The shift of starting point could alter partitions all along, but partition count until next original cut maybe. Suppose suffix pattern designed so starting one char earlier yields many segments? Difference in greedy partition counts between two starting positions of the same suffix with same k. Could be O(n). A single char could shift start position, and greedy partition count from position p vs p+1 can differ significantly? For a string with k distinct, number partitions from p vs p+1 maybe differ by man