n C, fast. Immediate extra maybe doubles to 520k, 68M, still okay (<1s-2s). Good.\n\nWait number c loops: n=10,000 * 26 = 260,000 yes. Very fine.\n\nNeed be careful `next_pos[x+1][d]` when x+1 n: row exists. Good.\n\nLet\'s attempt a brute-force verification for small mentally. Could include but final code only.\n\nLet\'s write final code now:\n\n```\nclass Solution:\n    def maxPartitionsAfterOperations(self, s: str, k: int) -> int:\n        n = len(s)\n        if k >= 26:\n            return 1\n        idx = [ord(ch)-97 for ch in s]\n        # suffix partitions\n        cnt=[0]*26\n        distinct=0\n        j=0\n        end=[n]*n\n        for i in range(n):\n            while j < n:\n                c=idx[j]\n                if cnt[c]==0 and distinct==k:\n                    break\n                if cnt[c]==0:\n                    distinct +=1\n                cnt[c]+=1\n                j +=1\n            end[i]=j\n            c=idx[i]\n            cnt[c]-=1\n            if cnt[c]==0:\n                distinct-=1\n        suf=[0]*(n+1)\n        for i in range(n-1,-1,-1):\n            suf[i] = 1 + (suf[end[i]] if end[i] < n else 0)\n        # original segments start and seg number\n        seg_start=[0]*n\n        seg_num=[0]*n\n        mask=0\n        L=0\n        seg=0\n        for i,c in enumerate(idx):\n            bit=1<<c\n            if (mask | bit).bit_count() > k:\n                seg += 1\n                L = i\n                mask = 0\n            mask |= bit\n            seg_start[i]=L\n            seg_num[i]=seg\n        # next positions\n        next_pos = [[n]*26 for _ in range(n+1)]\n        # row n already n\n        for i in range(n-1, -1, -1):\n            row = next_pos[i+1].copy()\n            row[idx[i]] = i\n            next_pos[i] = row\n\n        def first_exceed(start, x, c):\n            firsts = next_pos[start].copy()\n            d = idx[x]\n            if c != d:\n                if firsts[d] == x:\n                    firsts[d] = next_pos[x+1][d]\n                if firsts[c] > x:\n                    firsts[c] = x\n            firsts.sort()\n            return firsts[k]  # k <=25\n        ans=0\n        for x in range(n):\n            L0 = seg_start[x]\n            if x == L0 and L0 > 0:\n                L = seg_start[L0-1]\n            else:\n                L = L0\n            base = seg_num[L] if L < n else 0  # L is segment start\n            for c in range(26):\n                Y = first_exceed(L,x,c)\n                if Y == x:\n                    Z = first_exceed(x,x,c)\n                    cand = base + 2 + suf[Z]\n                else:\n                    cand = base + 1 + suf[Y]\n                if cand > ans:\n                    ans = cand\n        return ans\n```\n\nNeed consider `base = seg_num[L]`. If L maybe n? no. If n>=1. If L is previous segment start. seg_num[L] number partitions before segment starting at L. For L=0, seg_num[0]=0. For x inside segment and L=seg_start[x], seg_num[L] = seg_num[x]. Good.\n\nBut wait `seg_num` for L where L is segment start after a cut is assigned the new seg number. For positions in segment, same. `seg_num[L]` equals number partitions before L. Correct.\n\nLet\'s test example s="accca", k=2. k<26. Suffix computed. Original seg: all one, seg_num all0, L0=0. x=2 no move L=0 base0. c b: first_exceed returns Y=2==x, Z=4, cand=0+2+suf[4]. suf[4] for "a" with k2 =1. cand3. Good.\n\nWait earlier we computed Z=4, suf[4]=1. Formula base+2+suf[Z]=3. Yes.\n\nNow consider x at start with immediate and Z maybe n. Formula base+2+suf[n]=base+2. For "abc", k1, x=1 b->d, we computed Y=1, Z? first_exceed(start1,x1,d): modified from1 "d c", k1. next_pos[1] original b1,c2. d=b first b1==x shift next b n; add d at1. firsts d1,c2; k1 index1=2. Z=2. suf[2] original s[2]="c" =1. base before P0 +2+1=3. Modified "adc": partitions a,d,c =3. Good.\n\nWhat if Z=n? e.g., "ab", k1 x=1 b->d. P=0, first_exceed(0,1,d): original a0, d at1, Y=1 immediate. Z=first_exceed(st